 Nikhil Kamath asks Sam Altman for stock market advice, his luxury brand picks resurface 
 A prediction about the future of artificial intelligence, productivity and luxury spending has resurfaced online after Zerodha co-founder Nikhil Kamath shared an anecdote about a conversation with OpenAI CEO Sam Altman.
In a video clip that is now doing the rounds on social media, Kamath recalled interviewing Altman last year and asking him which stocks investors should consider buying.
According to Nikhil Kamath , Altman's response was based on the belief that artificial intelligence would significantly increase productivity, leaving people with greater financial means to spend on products that distinguish them from others.
 View this post on Instagram
 A post shared by Lucre (@lucreindia) 
Altman reportedly suggested looking at luxury companies such as Hermes and LVMH, the parent company of brands including Louis Vuitton, Dior and Tiffany & Co.
The underlying argument, as Kamath recalled it, was that as people became more productive and wealthier, they would increasingly seek to differentiate themselves through premium brands and luxury goods.
However, Kamath pointed out that the investment thesis has not translated into the expected market performance. He said shares of the luxury companies had fallen by around 50%, laughing as he reflected on the recommendation.
Using the example, Kamath questioned the gap between the optimism surrounding artificial intelligence and what is actually unfolding in the broader economy.
While the theoretical argument suggests that AI-driven productivity gains should create more wealth and spending power, he observed that the world still appears to be struggling, with the anticipated productivity improvements not yet translating into widespread economic gains.
The video has triggered reactions online, with users weighing in on Altman's views about AI and the technology's ability to deliver on its promises.
One user criticised Altman's assessment of artificial intelligence, arguing that he appears overly confident about OpenAI's capabilities. The commenter also claimed that OpenAI's models were not yet as capable as Anthropic's Claude.
The discussion comes amid growing scrutiny of the economic impact of artificial intelligence. While technology companies have made ambitious promises about productivity gains, investors and businesses are still assessing how quickly those improvements will translate into measurable economic benefits.
